meters from turkeys fortified border  with Syria are the ruins of an ancient  capital buried for millennia this is car  kamesh founded three and a half thousand  years ago once part of the hittite and  neo-assyrian empire emerging from the  earth sculptures of rulers and armies  recent excavations by Italian and  Turkish teams have uncovered some of the  finest mosaics of the ancient world  each city in Turkey has lot some of its  cultural heritage but this site is  something different we have found layer  upon layer of different civilizations  including a village dating back to 6000  BC the site straddles the modern border  40% of it lies unexcavated in Syria a  Turkish military base now sits on top of  the ancient Acropolis it's still a  designated active military zone but from  next year it will be an open-air museum  the regional authorities are confident  the area is now safe a few years ago  this area across the border in Syria  used to be ISIL country the black flag  flying high for all to see this part of  southeastern Turkey is the cradle of  civilization it's believed that car  kamesh is one of the places where  agriculture first appeared but as you  can imagine since the start of the  Syrian war the number of foreign  tourists that venture here has dropped  completely will the opening of this site  encourage people back this is no  ordinary archaeological site it's one  that can change the entire history of  the world in illuminate the late heat  heart era here on the side of the  Euphrates River  we are planning a huge National Park and  the week opening will be held next year  in May before today's frontiers divided  the ancient city this is what  archaeologists believe its walls may  have looked like for generations these  ruins have been hidden from view  indifferent to the rise and fall of  latest civilizations and the war that  still rages not far  from here in Syria near Parker al  Jazeera car kamesh on the turkey Syrian  border
 

